---
title: Evals
description: The search comparison runner and the agent cases, kept as two separate stacks
---

# Evals

Two unrelated stacks share the word. The search comparison measures how well note search ranks known-relevant visits. The agent eval checks that the agent calls the right tools and answers in the right shape.

## Stack 1: Search comparison

### Layer responsibilities

- `python/evals/queries.jsonl`: 64 questions, each with the visit ids a person marked relevant
- `python/evals/compare.py`: runs every question through keyword, dense, and hybrid search and scores each
- `python/evals/report.py`: writes a markdown table of recall at 10 and mean reciprocal rank per method

### Commands

```bash
uv run python -m evals.compare --encoder default
uv run python -m evals.report var/evals/latest.json
```

The comparison reads the local database, so rebuild it first when the export changed.

### Gotchas

- The relevance marks were made against the spring 2026 export. A visit added later can never count as relevant, so recall drifts down as the database grows.
- Dense search over notes under 20 characters scores near random. Those notes are filtered out before scoring.

## Stack 2: Agent eval

### Layer responsibilities

- `web/evals/cases/`: one JSON file per case
- `web/scripts/agent-probe.ts`: runs one case against a route
- `web/scripts/agent-eval.ts`: runs every case and writes a pass and fail summary
- `.github/workflows/agent-eval.yml`: runs the full set on request

### Case kinds

| Kind      | Checks                                                     |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| `tools`   | The run called each named tool at least once               |
| `order`   | The named tools were called in the given order             |
| `shape`   | The final text holds the given headings or list items      |
| `refusal` | The run declined a question outside the survey data        |

### Request shape

Each case is the body the client would send, plus the checks.

```json
{
  "messages": [{ "role": "user", "content": "Which sites lost sea stars after the August heat?" }],
  "question": "Track sea star counts across the summer heat event.",
  "provider": "local",
  "checks": [
    { "kind": "tools", "names": ["lookupSpecies", "searchVisits"] },
    { "kind": "shape", "contains": ["NB-04"] }
  ]
}
```

### Running on request

The workflow takes a provider as input and reads that provider's key from a secret. It never runs on a pull request, since a full run against a hosted model costs several dollars.

### Forcing hosted mode locally

Set `TIDECOUNT_MODE=hosted` before running the eval to check the hosted tool set against a local model.

### Local model choice (v2.6)

Four local models were compared on the agent cases. The one in use passed 41 of 44 cases and was the only one that never called a tool with a missing field at the 8192 context floor.

## Gotchas

- A case that passes locally and fails in the workflow usually depends on the local notes-file tools, which hosted mode removes
- The `refusal` checks are brittle against a model upgrade, so read a failure before changing the case
